=================================================subject==========================================
Subject solves this
const subject = new Subject<number>();
Subject is:
Observable (can subscribe) + 
Observer (has next())
📌 Acts like an event emitter
Example
const subject = new Subject<number>();

subject.subscribe(v => console.log('A', v));
subject.subscribe(v => console.log('B', v));

subject.next(1);
subject.next(2);

Output:
A 1
B 1
A 2
B 2

What is a Subject (in one line)

👉 Subject = Observable + Observer
You can emit values using next()
You can subscribe to it like an Observable
It does NOT store any value

Key rules of Subject
✔ No initial value
✔ Emits values only to current subscribers
❌ Does NOT replay old values
❌ Late subscribers miss previous emissions
====================================================================================================================================================
EX: why last subscriber miss value:
Code example (step-by-step)
        import { Subject } from 'rxjs';
        const subject = new Subject<number>();
        // Subscriber 1
        subject.subscribe(value => {
          console.log('Subscriber 1:', value);
        });
        subject.next(1);
        subject.next(2);
        // Subscriber 2 (LATE)
        subject.subscribe(value => {
          console.log('Subscriber 2:', value);
        });
        subject.next(3);

        🔍 Output
        Subscriber 1: 1
        Subscriber 1: 2
        Subscriber 1: 3
        Subscriber 2: 3

5️⃣ WHY late subscribers miss old values?
Behind the scenes 🧠
Subject does not store emitted values
When you call next(value):
It immediately pushes the value to currently subscribed observers
If no one is listening → value is lost forever

Subject does not have memory.
It only emits values to active subscribers, so late subscribers miss previously emitted values.

7️⃣ When should you use Subject?
✔ Button clicks
✔ Events (mouse, keyboard)
✔ Notifications
✔ Real-time streams where past data doesn’t matter
❌ App state
❌ Shared data that new components need immediately
