*ngFor does NOT iterate a single object directly.
Angular’s *ngFor works only with iterables (arrays).
So when you do this 👇
<div *ngFor="let item of user | keyvalue">
  {{ item.key }} : {{ item.value }}
</div>

You are not looping the object —
you are looping an array created from the object by the keyvalue pipe.

What actually happens step-by-step
1️⃣ Your object
user = {
  name: 'Nazeer',
  age: 25,
  role: 'Developer'
};

This is a plain JavaScript object, not iterable.
❌ This will NOT work:
<div *ngFor="let item of user">...</div>

Because objects don’t have:
index
length
iterator (Symbol.iterator)

2️⃣ What keyvalue pipe does
Angular’s keyvalue pipe converts an object into an array
user | keyvalue
Becomes something like:
[
  { key: 'name', value: 'Nazeer' },
  { key: 'age', value: 25 },
  { key: 'role', value: 'Developer' }
]

✅ Now it is iterable → *ngFor can loop.
3️⃣ *ngFor iterates the array
<div *ngFor="let item of user | keyvalue">
  {{ item.key }} : {{ item.value }}
</div>

Output:
name : Nazeer
age : 25
role : Developer

So why use ngFor here?
Because:
You want to display dynamic object properties
You don’t know keys in advance
You want reusable UI logic

Without ngFor (hardcoded ❌)
<p>Name: {{ user.name }}</p>
<p>Age: {{ user.age }}</p>
<p>Role: {{ user.role }}</p>


This breaks if:
New properties are added
API response changes

=================================================================================================================================================================
Then how do we loop objects in Angular? 🤔
✅ Solution 1: Use keyvalue pipe (MOST COMMON)

Angular provides a built-in pipe for this.
<div *ngFor="let item of user | keyvalue">
  {{ item.key }} : {{ item.value }}
</div>
✔ Converts object → iterable
✔ Safe and recommended
✔ Interview-friendly answer

✅ Solution 2: Convert object to array in TS
userArray = Object.entries(this.user);
<div *ngFor="let item of userArray">
  {{ item[0] }} : {{ item[1] }}
</div>
Used when:
You want full control
Complex transformations needed

✅ Solution 3: Map / Set
userMap = new Map([
  ['name', 'Nazeer'],
  ['age', 25]
]);
<div *ngFor="let item of userMap">
  {{ item[0] }} : {{ item[1] }}
</div>

